germany has started its journey back to  a sort of normal life but it is slow  progress  here in dusseldorf schools will open for  a couple of year groups  limited numbers temperature checks  disinfected rooms multiplied by 3 over  13.  at this international school they've  already welcomed back a handful of  students  but they know the next step will be much  more demanding  we've set up the social distancing we've  kept the two meters not one and a half  meters two meters  separation and we have fixed all of the  signs  to reinforce the social distancing it's  going to be hugely challenging for  children coming back  after months of not seeing their  their peers except virtually but i think  we will manage it  some students have already said they  won't be coming in others  will return keen to use school as a  touchstone to regular life  we have to try to have some kind of  formality  and try to kind of support the economy  so it's really finding a midway between  all opinions and  making sure that we do our best to not  spread it anymore  for parents at least for the parents of  children returning to the classroom  this also feels like a milestone in the  story of this virus  it's a huge impact of course for for the  well-being of the children  for a long time i mean for shorter time  they of course they're fine being home  but for a longer period of time  i think it's really important to get the  children back to school  germany has already eased restrictions  small shops are trading  playgrounds and museums will open  there's a hint here  of how life used to be so the schools  are going back  some of the shops are open and there are  plenty of people around if it  weren't for the masks you might think  that this was  life as normal but there are those who  think that germany has gone  too far too fast in loosening its  lockdown  change will vary between regions some  will ease rules  quicker than others here they think  they've got the balance  just about right we have declining  numbers and i believe it's important  because germany doesn't just consist of  students  but also economic and social structure  it's important we think of how we can  return  step by step to a normality but while  adhering to rules about hygiene  the fear is of the unknown of what will  happen in germany and beyond  when countries relax their rules virtual  classrooms  will be replaced by real ones many here  and across europe will be watching to  see what happens next  adam parsons sky news dusseldorf
 

